{0 Making a game on CamlCast}

A step-by-step guide to building a game on this engine, from an empty directory
to something you can walk around in. Every step adds one feature, shows the code
that uses it, says what you should see when you run it, and names the demo that
does nothing else — so anything unclear here can be read at full length in a file
short enough to finish in a sitting.

{b Who this is for.} Anyone who can write OCaml. No graphics background is
assumed: the engine draws the picture, and what a game hands it is geometry and
values. If you want to know {e how} the picture gets drawn, that is the other
page — {{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine from scratch}.

{b What you will have built.} A world of rooms joined by doorways, dressed in
materials of your own, with pictures on the walls and figures standing in it,
doors that open, a HUD over the top, a crosshair that knows what it is pointing
at, and a level that builds itself as the player walks into it.

{b The route.} Steps 1 to 4 build one room and dress it — geometry, materials,
the air. Steps 5 to 7 make a world of rooms: doorways, sloped floors, the open
sky. Steps 8 to 11 put things in it: pictures, see-through walls, doors, art
from files. Steps 12 to 15 make it a game: your own state, input, a HUD, a
crosshair that answers. Steps 16 to 18 let it change: rebuilt rooms, growth, a
record of the route walked. Steps 19 to 21 tune it, test it and ship it.

{b The complete programs are compiled.} Four steps below are whole programs,
and each lives in this repository's [examples/] directory, built with
everything else precisely so that it cannot drift from the engine: step 1 is
[examples/room.ml], step 5 is [examples/doorways.ml], step 12 is
[examples/game.ml] and step 13 is [examples/rebind.ml]. Every other block is a
{e fragment}, and says at its head which earlier step's names it borrows.

{1 Four things to know before the first line}

These surprise everyone, and every one of them is load-bearing.

{2 There is no level file format}

No JSON, no TOML, no map editor, no custom binary. {b A level is OCaml code.} A
room is a value built by calling functions, and a world is a list of those with a
list of links beside it. That is the whole of it — so a level is type-checked, a
level generator is an ordinary function, and half a level can be shared between
two others by giving it a name.

The only things read from disk are PNG and JPEG, through
{!Camlcast.Texture.load}, {!Camlcast.Image.load} and {!Camlcast.Font.make}'s
atlas. See {{!files} Art from files}.

{2 The engine holds no content}

Not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it has are the types those things
are values of. So every step below supplies its own {!Camlcast.Texture},
{!Camlcast.Material} and {!Camlcast.Atmosphere}, and two games built on this
engine need not look remotely alike.

{2 Two ways of failing}

Which one you get says what kind of mistake it was.

{ul
{li {b Things that can fail at run time return a result} —
    [('a, [ `Msg of string ]) result]. {!Camlcast.Engine.with_window},
    {!Camlcast.Engine.run_world}, {!Camlcast.Engine.run},
    {!Camlcast.Texture.load}, {!Camlcast.Image.load}, {!Camlcast.Asset.path},
    {!Camlcast.Framebuffer.make}. A missing file is one of these.}
{li {b Authoring mistakes raise [Invalid_argument]} — {!Camlcast.World.make},
    {!Camlcast.World.link}, {!Camlcast.World.add_room},
    {!Camlcast.World.open_doorway}, {!Camlcast.Room.doorway},
    {!Camlcast.Font.make}, {!Camlcast.Atmosphere.make}, {!Camlcast.Sky.make},
    {!Camlcast.Room.rectangle}, {!Camlcast.Transform.between},
    {!Camlcast.Texture.noise}, {!Camlcast.Texture.generate},
    {!Camlcast.Image.make}, {!Camlcast.Framebuffer.offscreen},
    {!Camlcast.Key.of_scancode}, {!Camlcast.Player.make},
    {!Camlcast.Player.turn}, {!Camlcast.Player.pitch_by},
    {!Camlcast.Player.traverse}. A doorway linked to nothing, two rooms with the
    same name, a doorway wider than the wall it is cut into, air lit from
    nowhere, a camera facing a [nan], a buffer with no size — these are bugs in
    the level or in the game, not conditions to handle, and they stop the
    program with a message naming what was wrong.}}

{!Camlcast.Framebuffer.make} is on both lists, and honestly: the result is the
renderer's, the raise is yours.

So [World.make] crashing is the engine telling you the level is malformed. Read
the message; it names the room and the doorway.

{2 Rooms are wound counterclockwise}

Author a room's boundary so that, walking each wall from its first endpoint to
its second, the inside of the room is to the {e left on paper} — the direction
these pages call counterclockwise. (On the screen's map, where y grows
downward, the same loop reads clockwise to the eye; trust the rule, not the
picture. The one full statement of it lives at the top of {!Camlcast.Room},
and everything else, including this page, defers to it.) Give a doorway's
endpoints in that same direction. A link between two doorways is derived from
their four endpoints and nothing else, and {!Camlcast.Transform.between} pairs
them in reverse — because the two rooms describe the same opening from
opposite sides. Wind one the other way and the neighbour arrives inside out.

If a room looks black from the inside, this is why. The shapes the engine
builds for you — {!Camlcast.Room.rectangle},
{!Camlcast.Room.regular_polygon} — cannot be wound wrong; walls you write out
by hand can.

{1 Step 0 — A project that depends on the engine}

The engine is not on opam yet, so pin it from git.

{@sh[
mkdir mygame && cd mygame
opam switch create . 5.5.0                # or use a switch you already have
eval $(opam env --switch=. --set-switch)  # so the pin lands in it, whatever
                                          # OPAMSWITCH was pointing at before
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git
]}

{b If the pin fails on macOS}, it is opam's depext check: Homebrew ships
[sdl2-compat] under the name [sdl2] and opam cannot see it. Install the system
libraries yourself and tell opam to stop looking:

{@sh[
brew install sdl2 sdl2_image
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git --no-depexts
]}

On Debian or Ubuntu, opam resolves [libsdl2-dev] and [libsdl2-image-dev] through
apt without help. On Windows, use the MSYS2 environment.

Three files. [dune-project]:

{[
(lang dune 3.21)
(name mygame)
]}

[bin/dune]:

{[
(executable
 (name main)
 (libraries camlcast))
]}

And [bin/main.ml], empty for now.

{b Check it:} [dune build] finishes with no output.

{1:room Step 1 — One room you can walk in}

{b Goal.} A window, four walls, a floor and a ceiling, and mouse-look movement
inside them.

Five values and you are standing in a room. Here is the whole program — it is
[examples/room.ml] in the engine's repository, compiled beside the engine, and
the example in its README, all one file:

{@ocaml[
open Camlcast

(* A pattern is a pure function from a texel coordinate to a colour. This one is
   a check; Color.level scales all three channels together, so it moves the
   brightness without touching the hue. Both coordinates and the levels are
   0 .. 255 — the range every pattern computes in. *)
let checker ~color ~u ~v =
  Color.level color (if ((u / 16) + (v / 16)) land 1 = 0 then 240 else 170)

let stone =
  Material.make
    ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 150 150 160)))

let ground =
  Material.make
    ~pattern:(Texture.generate (checker ~color:(Color.rgb 116 110 98)))

let world =
  let height = 4. in
  (* Distances are in cells: one cell is one texture repeat, and the eye
     stands half a cell up, so a 12-cell room under a 4-cell ceiling reads as
     a hall. *)
  let floor = Plane.horizontal 0. in
  let room =
    Room.make
      ~floor:(Room.floor ~plane:floor ~material:ground)
      ~ceiling:(Room.roof ~plane:(Plane.above floor height) ~material:stone)
      (* The axis-aligned box, from two opposite corners. *)
      (Room.rectangle ~height ~material:stone (Vec.make (-6.) (-6.))
         (Vec.make 6. 6.))
  in
  (* The air of an unremarkable day. Step 3 of the guide is about your own. *)
  World.make
    ~rooms:[ ("room", room) ]
    ~links:[] ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default
    ~spawn:("room", Vec.make (-4.5) 0.)

let () =
  (* [with_window] opens the window, hands it over, and closes it again when
     this is done with it — on the way out of an error just the same. *)
  match Engine.with_window (fun window -> Engine.run_world window world) with
  (* How the run ended matters only to a program that plays a second one.
     This one has the single room above and nothing to go back to. *)
  | Ok _ending -> ()
  | Error (`Msg m) ->
      prerr_endline m;
      exit 1
]}

{b Check it:} [dune exec bin/main.exe]. A window opens on a square room with a
checked floor. {b W}/{b A}/{b S}/{b D} walk, the mouse looks around, {b F11} is
fullscreen, {b Esc} quits.

{{image:images/minimal.png}The square room those six values make: a checked floor, four walls, and the far one already fading into the air}

{2 What each piece is}

{ul
{li {!Camlcast.Vec} is a plain 2-D point. One unit is one "cell" — the eye sits
    at {!Camlcast.Config.eye_height}, half a unit up, so a wall four units tall
    is about eight times eye height.}
{li {!Camlcast.Texture.generate} turns a function of [~u] and [~v] into a square
    pattern that tiles {e once per world unit}. It is a density, not a
    resolution: a 64-texel texture on a wall six units long repeats six times.}
{li {!Camlcast.Material.make} wraps a pattern into the thing a wall wears.}
{li {!Camlcast.Plane} is an elevation over the room, [z = a*x + b*y + c].
    {!Camlcast.Plane.horizontal} is the flat case and {!Camlcast.Plane.above}
    raises one by a fixed amount — used for the ceiling here so that when the
    floor stops being flat in step 6, the roof follows it by construction.}
{li {!Camlcast.Room.make} takes a wall list, a floor and a ceiling, and the
    two vertical bounds read the same way: {!Camlcast.Room.val-floor} names a
    plane and a material, {!Camlcast.Room.roof} the same — or
    {!Camlcast.Room.open_sky} instead of a roof, which is step 7. A floor is
    always a surface; a ceiling has to say which of the two things overhead
    it is, so that a room open to the sky cannot forget to say which sky.}
{li {!Camlcast.Room.rectangle} is the axis-aligned box — the one shape common
    enough to earn a shortcut. Walls written out by hand come in step 4.}
{li {!Camlcast.World.make} names the rooms, links their doorways (none yet),
    gives the world its air, and says where the player starts. The spawn
    faces along the positive x axis; a game that wants another facing runs
    the loop itself with {!Camlcast.Player.spawn}'s [~angle] — step 12.}
{li {!Camlcast.Engine.with_window} opens the window, and closes it again when
    what it hands the window to has finished with it.}
{li {!Camlcast.Engine.run_world} runs the loop on that window until the player
    quits.}}

{b If a room is ever black from the inside}, its walls are wound the wrong
way round — reverse them. It cannot happen to this one:
{!Camlcast.Room.rectangle} winds its four walls for you, whichever corners it
is given.

{b Read next:} [demo/masonry.ml] is this, with four materials and a post in it.

{{image:images/masonry.png}A tiled green wall and an oak post: one pattern function, worn at two colours}

{1 Step 2 — Colour, patterns and materials}

{b Goal.} Walls that look like something.

A texel is a {!Camlcast.Color.t}, so one wall can have more than one colour in
it: brick with grey mortar between it, lead around a pale pane. The idiom that
makes patterns reusable is {b taking the colours before [~u] and [~v]}, so that a
partial application is a pattern:

{@ocaml[
let hash = Texture.hash

(* Running bond: courses 16 texels high, every other one shifted half a brick so
   the vertical joints never line up. The mortar is its own colour and not a
   paler brick — which is the whole reason a texel carries a colour. *)
let brick ~color ~mortar ~u ~v =
  let course = v / 16 in
  let u = (u + if course land 1 = 0 then 0 else 16) mod Texture.default_size in
  if v mod 16 < 2 || u mod 32 < 2 then Color.level mortar 210
  else Color.level color (225 + (hash (u / 32) course mod 30))

let solid f = Material.make ~pattern:(Texture.generate f)

let red =
  solid (brick ~color:(Color.rgb 200 70 70) ~mortar:(Color.rgb 188 182 172))

let grey =
  solid (brick ~color:(Color.rgb 120 120 128) ~mortar:(Color.rgb 188 182 172))
]}

Two materials, one function. {!Camlcast.Color.level} is the join between the two
halves: patterns compute in the 0..255 that {!Camlcast.Texture.noise} and the
hashes beside it speak, and [level] turns that back into a colour by scaling all
three channels together.

{!Camlcast.Texture.noise} is a value-noise field that {b wraps at the texture's
size}, which is what lets a pattern have no courses or joints at all — an
unwrapped field would put a hard seam down every wall, one per world unit, and a
pattern with no lines of its own has nothing to hide it behind.

{b Check it:} swap [stone] for [red] in step 1 and look closely at a course line.
The mortar is grey, not pink.

{b Read next:} [demo/patterns.ml] has seven patterns as pure functions and
[demo/surfaces.ml] fills their colours in; [demo/masonry.ml] shows four at once.

{1 Step 3 — The air and the light}

{b Goal.} Distance and shape, which come from the air rather than from the walls.

An {!Camlcast.Atmosphere} is one per {e world}, and is most of what tells one
place from another. Step 1 borrowed {!Camlcast.Atmosphere.default}; every
argument of {!Camlcast.Atmosphere.make} defaults to that air, so your own
names only what is different about your place:

{@ocaml[
(* A fogbound cellar: the fade closes in at five cells and lands on black,
   and the light has no direction worth speaking of. Everything unsaid —
   the light's own direction, the floor of the fade — stays the default's. *)
let cellar_air =
  Atmosphere.make
    ~haze:(Color.rgb 6 6 8)
    ~fog_distance:5.
    ~ambient:0.85 ~directional:0.15 ()
]}

The six, with their units and ranges (each is also on
{!Camlcast.Atmosphere.make}, beside its default):

{ul
{li [haze : Color.t] — what distance fades things to. Default a dark
    blue-grey, [Color.rgb 24 24 32].}
{li [fog_distance : float] — how many {e cells} the fade takes to fully
    arrive; must be above [0.]. Small is a fogbound cellar, large a clear
    day. Default [12.].}
{li [min_brightness : float] — the floor the fade stops at, [0. .. 1.].
    Default [0.25].}
{li [light : Vec.t] — the direction light comes from; normalised for you.
    Default from the north-west.}
{li [ambient : float] — light arriving from everywhere, [0. .. 1.].
    Default [0.6].}
{li [directional : float] — how much more a face square to [light] gets,
    [0. .. 1.]. Default [0.4].}}

[ambient] and [directional] together decide how much a wall's angle matters:
all ambient and every wall is equally lit, all directional and a wall facing
away is dark. {b Keep their sum at or below [1.]} — the sum is what a face
square to the light is multiplied by, and past [1.] it blows out. Every
atmosphere in this repository keeps the sum at exactly [1.].

Out-of-range numbers are refused with [Invalid_argument] naming the argument
— a [fog_distance] of [0.] is a division by zero wearing a trench coat, so
{!Camlcast.Atmosphere.make} does not let it in the door.

{b Check it:} set [~fog_distance:4. ()] and walk backwards. The far wall turns
into the haze colour — set [~haze] to something lurid and you can watch it
happen.

{2:units The units, once and for all}

Four scales cover the whole engine, and every numeric argument names its own
in its doc:

{ul
{li {b Cells} — all distances and heights. One cell is one texture repeat
    along a wall or a floor; the eye stands {!Camlcast.Config.eye_height},
    half a cell, above the floor.}
{li {b Radians} — all angles, on {!Camlcast.Vec.of_angle}'s reckoning.}
{li {b 0 .. 255} — colour channels and alpha, the range patterns compute
    in.}
{li {b [0. .. 1.]} — fades and fractions: glow, brightness, the two shading
    terms above.}}

The glossary in {{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine} carries
the longer story, and {!Camlcast.Config} the constants these scales hang
off.

{b Read next:} [demo/haze.ml].

{{image:images/haze.png}A corridor fading into its own haze, brightest on the faces the light falls on}

{1 Step 4 — Shapes without writing out every wall}

{b Goal.} Rooms that are not squares.

With [height] and [stone] as in step 1, three wall-list builders cover the
common shapes — and anything else is walls written out by hand:

{@ocaml[
(* An open run of walls, or a closed loop with ~closed:true. This is the
   hand-wound case, so the winding rule from the top of the page applies. *)
let corridor =
  Room.path ~height ~material:stone
    [ Vec.make 0. 0.; Vec.make 8. 0.; Vec.make 8. 3.; Vec.make 14. 3. ]

(* A hexagonal room — or, at a small radius, a pillar standing in one. *)
let hexagon =
  Room.regular_polygon ~center:(Vec.make 0. 0.) ~radius:8. ~sides:6
    ~rotation:0. ~height ~material:stone

(* A low square post, standing in whatever room's list it joins. *)
let post =
  Room.regular_polygon ~center:(Vec.make 2.5 3.5) ~radius:0.7 ~sides:4
    ~rotation:0.4 ~height:2.6 ~material:stone
]}

All of these — and {!Camlcast.Room.rectangle} from step 1 — return a plain
wall list, so they concatenate with walls you wrote by hand, and a room built
this way is no different from one written out segment by segment. Nothing
says a room's walls must form one loop: a pillar is four more walls in the
same list, wound the same way. And each wall carries its own height, so a low
wall drops below its neighbours and you see over it.

{b Read next:} [demo/masonry.ml] puts a post in a square room.

{1:doorways Step 5 — Two rooms and a doorway}

{b Goal.} A second room you can see into and walk through.

This is the engine's defining feature, so it is worth being slow about. A
{!Camlcast.Room} is authored in {b its own coordinates} and knows nothing of
where it sits. A {!Camlcast.World} names rooms and links their doorways, and a
link is a rigid rotation and translation derived from the four endpoints — so two
rooms may sit on top of each other numerically and still be separate places, and
a corridor can return you somewhere it could not possibly go.

{!Camlcast.Room.doorway} cuts a gap in the middle of the wall that would have run
from [a] to [b], and hands back {b both} the two jambs left either side {b and}
the threshold that fills the gap. Taking them together is what stops a boundary
and its openings from drifting apart:

With [ground] and [stone] as in step 1, [red] as in step 2, and [soffit] any
material you like the ceiling in (the demos keep one by that name — the
underside of a roof is a soffit). The whole program, materials and all, is
[examples/doorways.ml]:

{@ocaml[
(* Taller than step 1's rooms: this one is its own program. *)
let height = 5.

(* One room description, called twice below to build its two instances. *)
let chamber () =
  (* Wound as the rule at the top of the page says; doorway endpoints run the
     same way as the boundary they are cut from. *)
  let sw = Vec.make (-3.5) 0.
  and se = Vec.make 3.5 0.
  and ne = Vec.make 3.5 9.
  and nw = Vec.make (-3.5) 9. in
  let jambs, back =
    Room.doorway ~name:"back" ~width:2.6 ~opening:3. ~height ~material:red sw se
  in
  (* Partial application is the wall-list idiom: fix what the walls share,
     write only their endpoints. *)
  let wall = Room.wall ~height ~material:red in
  Room.make ~thresholds:[ back ]
    ~floor:(Room.floor ~plane:(Plane.horizontal 0.) ~material:ground)
    ~ceiling:(Room.roof ~plane:(Plane.horizontal height) ~material:soffit)
    (jambs @ [ wall se ne; wall ne nw; wall nw sw ])

let world =
  (* The hub is a hexagon of radius 8: corner k sits k sixths of a turn
     around it, and side k runs from corner k to the next. Sides 0 and 5
     become doorways; sides 1 to 4 stay walls. *)
  let corner k =
    let angle = float_of_int k *. Float.pi /. 3. in
    Vec.make (8. *. cos angle) (8. *. sin angle)
  in
  let gate k name =
    Room.doorway ~name ~width:2.6 ~opening:3. ~height ~material:stone (corner k)
      (corner ((k + 1) mod 6))
  in
  let right_jambs, right = gate 0 "right"
  and left_jambs, left = gate 5 "left" in
  let side k =
    Room.wall ~height ~material:stone (corner k) (corner ((k + 1) mod 6))
  in
  let hub =
    Room.make ~thresholds:[ right; left ]
      ~floor:(Room.floor ~plane:(Plane.horizontal 0.) ~material:ground)
      ~ceiling:(Room.roof ~plane:(Plane.horizontal height) ~material:soffit)
      (List.concat [ right_jambs; left_jambs; List.map side [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ] ])
  in
  World.make
    ~rooms:[ ("hub", hub); ("east", chamber ()); ("west", chamber ()) ]
    ~links:
      [
        (("hub", "right"), ("east", "back")); (("hub", "left"), ("west", "back"));
      ]
    ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default
    ~spawn:("hub", Vec.make (-6.) 0.)
]}

{b Check it:} two doorways in a hexagonal hub, each with a room beyond. The
two rooms beyond are {e the same description}, [chamber], called twice — what
puts one instance off to the right and one off to the left, each turned to
meet the wall it opens onto, is the doorway and nothing else. Neither call
said where its room sits, because a room never knows.

{2 Three arguments worth naming}

[~width] is how wide the gap is, [~opening] is how tall, and [~height] is the
height of the wall being cut — which becomes the threshold's {b lintel}, the
strip of wall left standing above the opening. Without a lintel you would see
over the top of a closed door.

{2 What [World.make] refuses}

All of these raise [Invalid_argument] with a message naming the offender:

{ul
{li an unknown room or doorway name;}
{li two rooms with the same name, or two doorways with the same name in one
    room;}
{li a doorway linked twice, or a doorway nothing links to;}
{li linked doorways that differ in length or in height;}
{li linked doorways that disagree about a door — a leaf on one side and an
    opening on the other would be a door you could see through from behind.}}

What it does {e not} check is whether the two floors meet. That is the next step.

{b Read next:} [demo/portals.ml].

{{image:images/portals.png}Two doorways in one wall, and through both of them the same red room with the same figure in it}

{1 Step 6 — Sloped floors and roofs}

{b Goal.} A floor that is not flat, and a doorway with no step in it.

A floor is a {!Camlcast.Plane}, [z = a*x + b*y + c], and so is a roof. Both tilt,
independently of each other:

{@ocaml[
let hall_floor = Plane.make ~a:0.11 ~b:0. ~c:0.
let hall_roof = Plane.make ~a:0.17 ~b:0. ~c:height
]}

The hall now climbs away from you and its roof climbs half again as fast, so the
room opens up ahead as you walk into it.

Each room has its own floor and nothing forces two of them to meet. Write two
planes out separately with the numbers that look right and they will differ by a
few thousandths where they meet, which reads as a step you walk into. Derive the
second from the first instead, with {!Camlcast.Plane.through}:

{@ocaml[
(* The two thresholds the link will join: [hall_onward] cut from the far end
   of the hall, [up_back] from the room beyond, exactly as in step 5.
   Room.across is the transform World.make will derive for that link, made
   available here so a floor can be carried across the doorway before the
   world that joins the rooms exists. *)
let onward = Room.across hall_onward up_back
let up_floor = Plane.through onward hall_floor
let up_roof = Plane.through onward hall_roof
]}

The same surface, expressed in the other room's terms. Now
{!Camlcast.World.seam_gap} is zero by construction rather than by care.

{b Check it:} walk through. Nothing catches underfoot, and the ceiling carries on
at the same angle.

{b Read next:} [demo/slopes.ml].

{{image:images/slopes.png}A tiled floor climbing the length of the hall to a doorway that stands higher than the floor underfoot, with the roof opening away from it as it goes}

{1 Step 7 — Open sky}

{b Goal.} A room with no roof.

The ceiling is chosen per room, so one room may have a roof while its
neighbour is open. A {!Camlcast.Sky} is built like an atmosphere: every
argument of {!Camlcast.Sky.make} defaults to {!Camlcast.Sky.default} — a
clear noon — so a sky names only what is different about it:

{@ocaml[
let noon = Sky.default

(* The same sky wound on a few hours: the sun gone round the compass
   (radians, on Vec.of_angle's reckoning) and dropped to the horizon. *)
let evening = Sky.make ~sun_azimuth:2.7 ~sun_height:0.06 ()

let yard =
  Room.make
    ~floor:(Room.floor ~plane:(Plane.horizontal 0.) ~material:ground)
    ~ceiling:(Room.open_sky noon)
    (Room.rectangle ~height:4. ~material:stone (Vec.make (-6.) (-6.))
       (Vec.make 6. 6.))
]}

Two of the sun's numbers are screen quantities, and their scale is worth
knowing before you reach for them. [sun_height] is a {e screen elevation}: a
level view spans only about [0. .. 0.4] from the horizon to the top of the
window, so [0.06] sits on the horizon, [0.5] rides high, and [1.] is above
the visible sky until the player looks up. [sun_radius] is the size of the
glow in view angle — roughly radians near the horizon — with useful values
around [0.2 .. 0.8]; it may not be zero, and {!Camlcast.Sky.make} refuses
one. [gradient] is how fast the horizon colour gives way to the zenith over
that same elevation scale, which is why values above [1.] are ordinary.

A sky is an {e infinitely far} backdrop: its colour depends only on the direction
looked in, never on where you stand. So it does not slide past as you walk — it
wheels as you turn and tilts as you look up.

{b The sky lights nothing.} What lights the walls is the world's
{!Camlcast.Atmosphere}, which is one per world. A room under a dusk sky is as
brightly lit as one under noon unless you change the air too.

{b Read next:} [demo/daylight.ml] puts two yards under two skies.

{{image:images/daylight.png}A low wall under an evening sky, with a second, warmer sky through the doorway in it}

{1 Step 8 — Pictures: decals and sprites}

{b Goal.} Something to look at that is not a wall.

An {!Camlcast.Image} is colour and alpha at whatever shape it was drawn — unlike
a {!Camlcast.Texture}, which is square and tiles a world cell because it is part
of a surface rather than a thing in its own right.

{@ocaml[
let poster =
  Image.make ~width:32 (fun ~u ~v ->
      if u < 2 || u > 29 || v < 2 || v > 29 then (Color.rgb 22 22 26, 255)
      else
        let du = float_of_int u -. 16. and dv = float_of_int v -. 16. in
        let d = Float.hypot du dv in
        if d > 8. && d < 12. then (Color.rgb 232 200 60, 255)
        else (Color.rgb 155 42 42, 255))
]}

{2 Decals — pictures fixed to a wall}

A decal is drawn as part of the wall, in the same pass and the same light, so
turn side-on to one and it foreshortens with the masonry it is painted over.
A fragment — [se] and [ne] are a wall's endpoints as in step 1, [panel] a
material, [painting] an image beside the [poster] above:

{@ocaml[
let hung =
  Room.wall ~height ~material:panel se ne
    ~decals:
      [ Room.decal ~along:3. ~z:1.7 ~half_width:1. ~half_height:1. painting;
        Room.decal ~along:6.5 ~z:1.7 ~half_width:0.8 ~half_height:1. poster ]
]}

[~along] is measured from the wall's {e first} endpoint and [~z] up from the
floor, so a decal is placed in the wall's own terms and stays put if the room
around it moves.

Two optional arguments matter. [~facing] puts the decal on one face of the wall
and not the other, which is what makes a chalk mark a mark rather than a hole.
[~glow] is how much of its own light it makes, from paint at [0.] to undimmable
at [1.] — that is what keeps a mark readable in a room whose light has failed,
without the light having to be implemented in any particular way to spare it.

{2 Sprites — pictures standing in the room}

A billboard: a position on the floor and a size, always facing you, composited by
depth after the walls are done — so it goes behind what is in front of it and in
front of what is not. With [figure], [barrel] and [lamp] images of your own:

{@ocaml[
Room.make
  ~sprites:
    [ Room.sprite ~size:1.8 ~image:figure (Vec.make 0. 1.8);
      Room.sprite ~size:0.9 ~image:barrel (Vec.make (-2.) (-2.5));
      (* ~base floats the foot above the floor — measured from the floor, so on
         a slope it rides with the ground. A hanging lamp, or a mote of dust. *)
      Room.sprite ~base:2.2 ~size:0.4 ~image:lamp (Vec.make 3. 3.) ]
]}

A sprite is as wide as its {e picture}, not as wide as it is tall, so a wide
short image is drawn wide and short. Cut sprites out against
{!Camlcast.Image.clear} so only the figure is drawn and not the box it came in.

{b Read next:} [demo/gallery.ml] for both side by side, [demo/floating.ml] for
[~base] and for choosing among frames made in advance, [demo/dust.ml] for a great
many of them moved every frame.

{{image:images/gallery.png}Two pictures hung on the wall, with figures standing in front of them}

{{image:images/floating.png}A sprite held off the floor, its frame picked from a strip made in advance}

{1 Step 9 — See-through walls}

{b Goal.} A grille you can see the next room through, and a window that tints it.

A texture can carry a per-texel alpha. A material wearing one is not opaque, and
the renderer defers it to the translucent pass:

{@ocaml[
let seen_through f = Material.make ~pattern:(Texture.generate_masked f)

(* Solid bars, clear holes. *)
let bars ~color ~u ~v =
  if u mod 16 < 5 || v mod 16 < 5 then
    (Color.level color (70 + (hash (u / 16) (v / 16) mod 30)), 255)
  else (Color.rgb 0 0 0, 0)

(* Solid lead, translucent panes: alpha 80 rather than 0 or 255. *)
let glass ~lead ~pane ~u ~v =
  let size = Texture.default_size in
  let mullion =
    u < 2 || u >= size - 2 || v < 2 || v >= size - 2
    || abs (u - (size / 2)) < 2
    || abs (v - (size / 2)) < 2
  in
  if mullion then (Color.level lead 150, 255) else (Color.level pane 235, 80)

let grille = seen_through (bars ~color:(Color.rgb 105 108 120))
]}

Nothing else changes — hang the material on an ordinary wall.
{!Camlcast.Material.opaque} is derived from the pattern's alpha, and that is what
routes it. Sprites and see-through walls are sorted {e together} by depth, which
is what lets a sprite cover a window in front of it and show through one behind
it.

{b Read next:} [demo/glass.ml].

{{image:images/glass.png}The next room seen through a grille and through a tinted window}

{1 Step 10 — Doors}

{b Goal.} A doorway with a leaf in it that opens and shuts.

A {!Camlcast.Door} has exactly two states. {b "Locked" is a game's word, not the
engine's} — the engine knows only whether something is drawn across the opening.
With [oak] a material, and [a] and [b] a wall's endpoints as before:

{@ocaml[
let oak_door = Door.make oak  (* Closed unless told otherwise *)
let standing_open = Door.make ~state:Door.Open oak

let jambs, threshold =
  Room.doorway ~name:"cellar" ~door:oak_door ~width:2.4 ~opening:3. ~height
    ~material:stone a b
]}

[~door] is optional and takes a {!Camlcast.Door.t} directly, so a helper that
threads "a door, or none" through several doorways forwards it as [?door] with
an option — which is what [demo/doors.ml] does.

A closed leaf is drawn and the step is refused; a door standing [Open] is neither
drawn nor felt, so it behaves exactly as an opening with no door in it. Both
sides of a link must agree about whether there is a door at all.

What the leaf is made of decides the rest, exactly as it does for a wall. A
material you can see through — {!Camlcast.Texture.generate_masked}, as in step 8
— makes a door the room beyond is drawn behind rather than instead of, and one
{!Camlcast.Sight} looks through; it still refuses the step, because being able to
see through a thing is not being able to walk through it. The same goes for the
lintel, the strip of wall left standing over an opening, which is how you get a
transom. {b Read next:} [demo/barred.ml] has one of each.

{{image:images/barred.png}A barred gate and the transom over it: you see through both and can walk past neither}

Opening one is a world rebuild, from your own update function. Which door?
{!Camlcast.Room.nearest_threshold} answers "the one the player is at":
nearest-wins within a reach, filtered to the thresholds that have a door
hung in them —

{@ocaml[
let at_hand world (player : Player.t) =
  Room.nearest_threshold ~within:3.5
    ~where:(fun t -> t.Room.door <> None)
    (World.room world player.Player.room)
    player.Player.pos

let toggled world (player : Player.t) threshold =
  let current =
    (Room.threshold_at (World.room world player.Player.room) threshold).Room.door
  in
  let next =
    match current with
    | Some { Door.state = Door.Closed; _ } -> Door.Open
    | _ -> Door.Closed
  in
  World.set_door world ~room:player.Player.room ~threshold next
]}

{!Camlcast.World.set_door} sets it on {e both} sides of the link at once, which
is what keeps the pair legal. A lock is then your own table of which doorways
refuse to be toggled.

{b Read next:} [demo/doors.ml], which has exactly that table.

{{image:images/doors.png}An oak door standing open, and through the opening the chamber it was shutting off}

{1:files Step 11 — Art from files}

{b Goal.} Textures and pictures read from PNG or JPEG instead of generated.

{@ocaml[
open Result_ext  (* for let* and let+ *)

let build () =
  let* tiles = Texture.of_asset "assets/tiles.png" in
  let* poster = Image.of_asset "assets/poster.png" in
  (* … build the world from them … *)
  Ok (world tiles poster)
]}

{!Camlcast.Texture.of_asset} is {!Camlcast.Asset.path} — which finds where the
file lives relative to the running executable — followed by
{!Camlcast.Texture.load}; {!Camlcast.Image.of_asset} is its twin. The [load]
pair is still there for a path you already hold.

A loaded texture and a generated one produce the same {!Camlcast.Texture.t}, and
the renderer has no way to ask which it was given. {b A texture must be square};
an image may be any rectangle. A file with no alpha of its own arrives solid.

{2 Where the files have to be}

{!Camlcast.Asset.path} searches {b relative to the executable}, never to the
source tree — because a shipped game has no source tree. In order:

{ol
{li [../Resources] — inside a macOS [.app] bundle;}
{li beside the executable — a tarball, or a Windows folder;}
{li one directory above it — which under dune is [_build/default];}
{li [share/], under the same prefix as the executable and named after it — an
    installed game: a binary called [wanderer] reads [share/wanderer].}}

So under dune, put your art in [assets/] at the project root and make sure dune
copies it into [_build]. The root [dune] file in this repository shows how:

{[
(alias
 (name default)
 (deps
  (alias_rec all)
  (source_tree assets)))
]}

Setting the [CAMLCAST_ASSETS] environment variable replaces that list entirely —
it looks there and nowhere else, and says so if it fails.

{b Load once, not per frame.} Put loaded art behind [lazy], or load it before the
world is built. Decoding a picture inside a frame is the thing the engine is
arranged to make unnecessary.

{b Read next:} [demo/loading.ml] puts a loaded pattern, decal and sprite beside
their generated counterparts.

{{image:images/loading.png}A pattern, a decal and a sprite read from PNG, beside the generated kind}

{1 Step 12 — Your own game state}

{b Goal.} Anything that is not just a world and a player walking it.

Everything so far used {!Camlcast.Engine.run_world}, which holds the only state
the engine can hold on its own. A game with phases, doors, a journal, a score or
a random seed uses {!Camlcast.Engine.run}, which runs a state of {e whatever
type you like}, described by an {!Camlcast.Engine.type-game} — built with
{!Camlcast.Engine.val-game} from the two things every game has and the four it
may not:

{ul
{li [update] — the next state, given how long the frame lasted, the movement
    asked for over it, and what the player is pressing and holding through it;}
{li [view] — which world and which player to draw this frame from;}
{li [overlay] — anything drawn over the finished picture (optional);}
{li [pointing] — whether the mouse is working a cursor over something you have
    drawn, rather than looking around (optional);}
{li [finished] — asked after every update; [true] ends the run (optional);}
{li [bindings] — what the player's controls are for: which walk, which look,
    which goes fullscreen, and which — {b if any} — ends the run. Step 13 is
    about this one. It defaults to {!Camlcast.Binding.default}, which binds
    {b no} key that leaves: that key belongs to whoever opened the window, and a
    game with screens in it wants it for closing them.}}

The whole program — the world, the air and a [fuse] length in seconds — is
[examples/game.ml]; here is the game in it:

{@ocaml[
open Camlcast

type phase = Waiting | Burning | Done
type t = { phase : phase; left : float; player : Player.t }

let start = { phase = Waiting; left = fuse; player = Player.spawn world }

let update state ~dt ~motion ~actions =
  (* Engine.step turns, pitches and walks in the right order and resolves
     collision. Nearly every game's update begins with this line. *)
  let player = Engine.step world state.player motion in
  match state.phase with
  | Waiting ->
      if Input.pressed actions (Input.Key Key.space) then
        { phase = Burning; left = fuse; player }
      else { state with player }
  | Burning ->
      let left = state.left -. dt in
      if left <= 0. then { phase = Done; left = 0.; player }
      else { state with left; player }
  | Done -> { state with player }

(* The world handed to the renderer need not be the one collision uses, and need
   not outlive the frame. Here the light fails as the fuse burns down. *)
let view state =
  let light =
    match state.phase with
    | Waiting -> 1.
    | Burning -> state.left /. fuse
    | Done -> 0.
  in
  (World.with_atmosphere world (air ~light), state.player)

let () =
  match
    Engine.with_window @@ fun window ->
    Engine.run window
      (Engine.game ~update ~view
         ~bindings:(Binding.make ~leave:[ Input.Key Key.escape ] ())
         ~finished:(fun state -> state.phase = Done)
         ())
      start
  with
  | Ok (_final, _ending) -> ()
  | Error (`Msg m) ->
      prerr_endline m;
      exit 1
]}

One more thing [run] hands you that [run_world] decides for itself: the
starting pose. [Player.spawn world] stands at the world's spawn facing along
the positive x axis; [Player.spawn ~angle:(Float.pi /. 2.) world] faces where
you say, on {!Camlcast.Vec.of_angle}'s reckoning.

{2 How a run ends}

[run] hands back the state it reached and an {!Camlcast.Engine.ending}:
[Closed] means the window was shut or the desktop asked the program to stop,
[Left] means the run ended on its own terms — [finished] said so, or the player
pressed something the binding table lists under [~leave]. Only something that
plays one run after another needs the difference; a launcher shows its menu
again after [Left] and stops after [Closed] — on the same window throughout,
which is what {!Camlcast.Engine.with_window} being a separate call buys it.
{!Camlcast.Engine.run_world} is
[run] over a bare world, returning the same answer; a program with only one
world to show has no use for it and can [ignore] it.

{b Nothing ends a run unless you say so.} {!Camlcast.Binding.default} binds no
key that leaves, because a game with a pause screen wants Escape for closing it
and would be poorly served by a window that quit underneath. [run_world] asks
for Escape, since a bare world has nothing else to end it with; [run] leaves it
to you, which is the [~bindings] line above.

{2 Time and focus}

Time passes only while the window has focus: an unfocused frame is handed
[dt = 0.] and no motion, so a game left behind another window is not given the
minutes it spent there when it comes back. A frame where [pointing] is true keeps
its clock but drops the motion.

{b Read next:} [demo/phases.ml].

{{image:images/phases.png}The room two thirds of the way through its fuse: the light going out of it, and the fog closing in as it does}

{1 Step 13 — Input}

{b Goal.} Keys and mouse buttons that mean something in your game — including
the ones that walk.

{b Input arrives as controls, not as actions.} The engine knows only that
something went down, came up, or has been held for so many seconds. What
"interact" or "journal" mean is your own table from the one to the other.

A control is a {!Camlcast.Key} or a mouse button. A key is a {e place on the
board} and not a letter: [Key.w] is the key above [Key.s] on any layout, so a
French player holding the key their board calls Z walks forward like everybody
else. {!Camlcast.Key.name} gives the layout's own name for it, which is what a
line of help text on screen should say.

{@ocaml[
let interact = Input.Key Key.e
let journal = Input.Key Key.tab
let primary = Input.Button Input.Left

let update state ~dt ~motion ~actions =
  let player = Engine.step world state.player motion in
  (* Four questions, over a key or a mouse button alike. *)
  let _ = Input.down actions interact in        (* is it down now? *)
  let _ = Input.pressed actions interact in     (* did it go down this frame? *)
  let _ = Input.released actions interact in    (* did it come up this frame? *)
  let held = Input.held_for actions interact in (* for how many seconds? *)
  (* held_for keeps its value for the one frame released is true, so a release
     can ask how long the hold was — a tap and a hold on one key. *)
  if Input.released actions interact then
    if held >= 0.4 then commit state player else tap state player
  else { state with player }
]}

Movement arrives already worked out: [~motion] carries the frame's forward,
strafe, turn and pitch, and [Engine.step] applies it in the right order — turn
and pitch before walking, so a frame that both turns and moves walks in the
direction it ends up facing.

{2 The keys that walk are yours too}

Where that motion came from is a {!Camlcast.Binding.t}, which you hand to
[run] beside the callbacks. {!Camlcast.Binding.default} is {b W}/{b A}/
{b S}/{b D}, the arrow keys, the mouse and {b F11} — and no key that ends the
run. It is a default and not a rule; the engine has no keys of its own. The
table below, run over a plain world, is [examples/rebind.ml]:

{@ocaml[
let bindings =
  Binding.make
    ~forward:
      {
        Binding.speed = 3.6 (* cells a second at full ask *);
        terms =
          [
            { Binding.source = Binding.Hold (Input.Key Key.i); weight = 1. };
            { Binding.source = Binding.Hold (Input.Key Key.k); weight = -1. };
          ];
      }
    ~leave:[ Input.Key Key.escape ] ()
]}

Every part not named keeps the default's — so the table above moves walking
forward and back onto {b I} and {b K}, adds Escape as a way out, and leaves
strafing, looking and fullscreen alone.

{b An axis adds up terms, and there are two kinds.} A {e rate} — a held control,
or an analog source like a gamepad stick — says how hard the player is asking,
between [-1] and [1]. Rates are summed, clamped to that range, and multiplied by
the axis's [speed] and by the length of the frame. The clamp matters: bind two
keys to one axis and holding both still walks at one speed, not two.

A {e displacement} — the mouse — says how far something {e has already moved}
during this frame. It is added on as it stands, because scaling it by the frame's
length would count the frame twice. That is why a mouse term's weight is a
sensitivity (radians per pixel) while a key's is just a sign.

{b Reading a table takes nothing but the table.} {!Camlcast.Binding.motion} is
pure — a table, a frame of {!Camlcast.Input.actions} and a duration in, a
movement out — so a test can ask what a given frame of keys would have done
without opening a window. [test/test_binding.ml] is that.

{b Read next:} [demo/controls.ml], which binds a second set of walking keys and
prints them with [Key.name].

{{image:images/controls.png}A line of help text naming the keys, and a meter part way through a hold}

{1 Step 14 — Drawing over the world: HUD, text and a cursor}

{b Goal.} A layer in front of everything.

[overlay] runs on the finished framebuffer after the world and before it reaches
the screen, so what it draws is in front of everything and clipped by nothing but
the buffer's own edges. The fragments in this step live inside a game whose
state keeps whatever they read — a [fuel] fraction, a [pointer] position.

{b The coordinates are the framebuffer's, not the window's.} The buffer is a
whole-number fraction of the window — see {!Camlcast.Renderer.internal_size},
capped at {!Camlcast.Config.max_render_height} — and the GPU stretches the
result. Measure everything from the buffer you are handed and the HUD keeps its
proportions at any window size.

{@ocaml[
let overlay fb state =
  let width = fb.Framebuffer.width and height = fb.Framebuffer.height in
  let unit = Int.max 3 (height / 60) in
  (* A band, blended so the world shows through it. *)
  Paint.rect fb ~x:0 ~y:(height - (8 * unit)) ~w:width ~h:(4 * unit)
    ~color:(Color.rgb 10 12 20) ~alpha:110;
  Paint.bar fb ~x:(2 * unit)
    ~y:(height - (7 * unit))
    ~w:(width - (4 * unit))
    ~h:(2 * unit)
    ~fraction:(state.fuel /. full)
    ~color:(Color.rgb 230 190 90);
  Paint.crosshair fb ~color:(Color.rgb 245 245 245)
]}

{!Camlcast.Paint} has clipped rectangles, bars, lines, dots, outlines, whole
images and sub-rectangles of them. Everything it draws is clipped against the
buffer, which is why {!Camlcast.Framebuffer.set} and
{!Camlcast.Framebuffer.blend} do not check where they are writing — the guards
live in [Paint] instead.

{2 Text}

{!Camlcast.Font} is a bitmap font on a fixed grid: a glyph's place in the atlas
is arithmetic on its code point and its advance is the cell width, so a font is a
picture and four numbers, with no metrics table to author beside the PNG or to
let drift out of step with it. Text is therefore monospaced.

{@ocaml[
let font =
  lazy
    (match
       let+ atlas = Image.of_asset "assets/font.png" in
       Font.make ~fallback:'\127' ~atlas ~width:6 ~height:10 ~first:32 ()
     with
    | Ok font -> font
    | Error (`Msg m) -> failwith ("could not read the font: " ^ m))

let overlay fb _state =
  let font = Lazy.force font in
  let column = (fb.Framebuffer.width * 2 / 3) - 12 in
  let body = String.concat "\n" (Font.wrap font paragraph ~width:column) in
  let tw, th = Font.measure font body in
  Paint.rect fb ~x:6 ~y:6 ~w:(tw + 12) ~h:(th + 12) ~color:(Color.rgb 12 14 22)
    ~alpha:190;
  Font.draw fb font body ~x:12 ~y:12 ~color:(Color.rgb 236 232 224)
]}

[measure] and [wrap] are pure functions of a string and the two cell dimensions,
so a panel can be sized from its text rather than guessed at. The atlas is drawn
white and multiplied by the colour [draw] is given — the same split
{!Camlcast.Texture} and {!Camlcast.Material} make, so one typeface dresses every
screen in the colour that screen wants. A line running off the edge is {e cut} by
[Paint]'s clipping rather than truncated by anything that measured it first.

{2 Letting go of the mouse}

Answering [true] to [pointing] releases the mouse capture and puts a real cursor
back on the screen. Its position arrives in [update] as [Input.pointer actions],
already in framebuffer coordinates — the ones [overlay] draws in, so no
conversion is needed:

{@ocaml[
let overlay fb state =
  if state.pointing then
    let x, y = state.pointer in
    Paint.rect fb ~x:(x - 2) ~y:(y - 2) ~w:4 ~h:4 ~color:(Color.rgb 250 250 250)
      ~alpha:255
  else Paint.crosshair fb ~color:(Color.rgb 245 245 245)
]}

{b Read next:} [demo/overlay.ml], [demo/text.ml], [demo/controls.ml].

{{image:images/overlay.png}A crosshair, and a meter along the bottom part filled}

{{image:images/text.png}A page of text wrapped to a column, measured, clipped at the edge and coloured}

{1 Step 15 — What the crosshair is on}

{b Goal.} Picking: knowing what the player is looking at, through doorways.

With [reach] a distance in cells, and [mark] and [collect] whatever your game
does with a wall and a sprite:

{@ocaml[
match Sight.look world player with
| Some { Sight.kind = Sight.Wall w; room; crossed; distance; _ }
  when crossed = 0 && distance <= reach ->
    (* w.index is which wall; w.along and w.z are where on it, in the wall's own
       coordinates — exactly what Room.decal wants, so aiming and marking need
       no conversion between them. *)
    mark ~room ~wall:w.index ~along:w.along ~z:w.z ~facing:w.facing
| Some { Sight.kind = Sight.Sprite s; room; _ } -> collect room s.index
(* A doorway, something out of reach, or nothing at all. The guard above means
   this catch-all has to be a wildcard rather than the Doorway case alone. *)
| Some _ | None -> ()
]}

[crossed] is how many doorways the ray went through to get there, and [room] is
which room the thing is in. Pass [~through] to look further; the default is one
doorway. [pose] is the player expressed in the {e target's} room frame, which is
what you project a HUD marker from.

{b Read next:} [demo/targets.ml] highlights what is under the crosshair through a
doorway; [demo/chalk.ml] turns a sighting straight into a decal.

{{image:images/targets.png}A green ring drawn round the barrel the crosshair is on, two rooms away through a doorway}

{1 Step 16 — Changing the world at run time}

{b Goal.} Something that moves.

A room is immutable, so a room that changes is a room that is built again. Three
functions, in increasing order of how much they rebuild — with [chalk] an image
and [motes] the game's own sprite list:

{@ocaml[
(* Only the sprite array is new — the walls, thresholds and both planes are the
   ones that were already there. This is how something drifts across a room. *)
let stirred room motes = Room.with_sprites room motes

(* One wall's decal list is new. This is how a mark appears. *)
let marked room =
  Room.add_decal room ~wall:3
    (Room.decal ~along:2. ~z:1.6 ~half_width:0.2 ~half_height:0.2 chalk)

(* And this is how either of them reaches the renderer. *)
let view state =
  let room = stirred (World.room world 0) (motes state) in
  (World.replace_room world ~room:0 ~replacement:room, state.player)
]}

{!Camlcast.World.replace_room} insists the thresholds are identical — same order,
names, endpoints and heights — and lets everything else change: walls, decals,
planes, sprites, doors. That is what keeps every index anything is holding
meaningful.

{b Build the changed world in [view], not in [update].} The world [view] hands
back is drawn and dropped, so collision in [update] can go on using the world as
authored and [update] stays a pure function of a clock and a pose. Rebuilding a
room every frame is what this costs — cheap for one room with six walls in it,
and a game with a hundred rooms rebuilds only the ones with something moving in
them.

{b The pictures must already exist.} Choosing among images made once at load is
the whole of animating a sprite. There is no frame counter in the engine, because
which frame it is at this moment is yours to decide.

{b Read next:} [demo/changing.ml], [demo/dust.ml], [demo/chalk.ml].

{{image:images/changing.png}A sign partway along the wall it is rebuilt onto every frame}

{{image:images/dust.png}A chamber of falling motes, every one of them moved this frame}

{{image:images/chalk.png}Three chalk marks on the wall, and a counter that agrees with them}

{1 Step 17 — Growing a world under the player}

{b Goal.} A level that builds itself as it is walked into.

A world can be {e grown} rather than authored. Three primitives, each of which
appends and does nothing else — so every index anything is holding keeps meaning
what it meant:

{ul
{li {!Camlcast.World.open_doorway} — cut a new doorway into an existing room;}
{li {!Camlcast.World.add_room} — append a room, returning its index;}
{li {!Camlcast.World.link} — join two doorways.}}

Between cutting a doorway and linking it, the doorway leads nowhere: the renderer
fills it with haze and collision treats it as solid. {b A half-built world is a
playable one}, rather than a crash waiting for someone to walk towards it.

The [extend] hook runs on a frame the player went {e through} a doorway on — not
every frame, so a generator may take its time — and returns the world to draw
from now on. Once per such frame rather than once per doorway: a fast step can
cross several, and what the hook is handed is the room the player finished in,
which is the one to build ahead of.

Verbatim from [demo/endless.ml] — whose [segment ~index ~back ~onward] builds
one corridor piece, and whose [way_on] finds a room's onward threshold if it
has one:

{@ocaml[
let rec build world ~room ~ahead =
  if ahead <= 0 then world
  else
    match way_on (World.room world room) with
    | Some index ->
        let portal = Option.get (World.portal world ~room ~threshold:index) in
        build world ~room:portal.World.to_room ~ahead:(ahead - 1)
    | None ->
        (* This room was built as a dead end. Give it a way on — the doorway it
           already has stays exactly where it was — and put a new dead end
           beyond it. *)
        let world =
          World.open_doorway world ~room
            ~opened:(segment ~index:room ~back:true ~onward:true)
        in
        let index = World.room_count world in
        let world, next =
          World.add_room world ~name:(named index)
            (segment ~index ~back:true ~onward:false)
        in
        let world = World.link world (room, "on") (next, "back") in
        build world ~room:next ~ahead:(ahead - 1)

let extend world (player : Player.t) =
  build world ~room:player.Player.room ~ahead:Config.max_portal_depth
]}

Then [Engine.run_world window ~extend world], exactly as the demo's [run]
line does.

{b Build exactly {!Camlcast.Config.max_portal_depth} doorways ahead}, which is
exactly how deep the renderer looks through them — build less and the player sees
the edge, build more and you paid for rooms nobody will reach.

{b Read next:} [demo/endless.ml].

{{image:images/endless.png}A corridor that is built ahead of you as you walk it}

{1 Step 18 — Knowing where the player walked}

{b Goal.} A record of the route, rather than a guess from the geometry.

{!Camlcast.Engine.move} is [step] that also hands back every doorway the frame
went through. With [record] your own fold over a crossing:

{@ocaml[
let update state ~dt:_ ~motion ~actions:_ =
  let moved = Engine.move world state.player motion in
  { player = moved.Player.player;
    stack = List.fold_left record state.stack moved.Player.crossings }
]}

A single frame can go through more than one — round a jamb, out through an
opening and back in through its twin, or all the way round a loop of rooms and
back where it started. Comparing room indices calls all of those nothing
happening; the crossing list does not.

Each {!Camlcast.Player.crossing} names both sides: [from_room],
[from_threshold], [to_room], [to_threshold], and the [onto] transform. Both sides
matter, because a room can be reached through more than one of its doorways and
only one of them is the way back.

That exactness is worth more than it sounds. A link is derived from two doorways
and from nothing else, so a corridor can return you somewhere it could not
possibly go — and a route home built from the crossings still arrives, because it
is a record of what was walked and not an inference from where the rooms are.

{b Read next:} [demo/trail.ml] builds a return route from them.

{{image:images/trail.png}Four ticks along the bottom: one for each doorway between here and the way out}

{1 Step 19 — Tuning}

{!Camlcast.Config} holds every tunable constant in one place. The ones a game
usually wants to think about:

{ul
{li [move_speed], [turn_speed] — how fast walking and turning are;}
{li [look_sensitivity], [pitch_sensitivity], [max_pitch] — the mouse, and how
    far up and down it can go;}
{li [eye_height] — half a unit, which is what makes a wall of height 4 feel as
    tall as it does;}
{li [collision_padding] — how close to a wall the player may get;}
{li [fov] — the horizontal field of view at [reference_aspect] (4:3);}
{li [max_render_height] — the cap on the software buffer, and so the trade
    between sharpness and frame rate;}
{li [max_portal_depth] — how many doorways deep the renderer looks, and so how
    far ahead an [extend] hook must build.}}

{b Resizing is Hor+.} The vertical field of view is fixed, so dragging the window
wider reveals more of the world to the sides rather than magnifying what was
already on screen, and pixels stay square at every shape. No size is cached
anywhere: each frame asks SDL for the current drawable size and builds a
{!Camlcast.Viewport} from it, which covers HiDPI displays and the fullscreen
transition without a resize event being handled at all.

{1 Step 20 — Testing a game with no window}

{b Goal.} Tests that run in CI.

Nothing in the rendering path needs a window. {!Camlcast.Framebuffer.offscreen}
builds a buffer with no streaming texture behind it — the texture was the only
part that needed one — and {!Camlcast.Renderer.draw_frame} fills a buffer with no
SDL call in it. So whole frames can be drawn and the pixels read back:

{@ocaml[
let fb = Framebuffer.offscreen ~width:320 ~height:240
let () = Renderer.draw_frame fb world (Player.spawn world)
let middle = Framebuffer.pixel fb ~x:160 ~y:120
]}

A trick worth stealing: to find where something landed, draw the frame twice —
once with it and once without — and take it to be the pixels that differ. That
says where it was drawn without claiming anything about the colour that fog and
shading left in it.

Worth checking about every level you author, which is what this repository's
[test_demos] checks about every demo:

{ul
{li that the player can stand where the world spawns them;}
{li that every room's walls enclose it;}
{li that every room is reachable through the links;}
{li that no floor steps across a doorway — {!Camlcast.World.seam_gap} is zero.}}

{!Camlcast.World.check} runs the structural half of that for you.

{1 Step 21 — Shipping}

A game built on this engine is an ordinary OCaml executable, plus its art. What
makes it awkward to distribute is SDL2 and the image codecs, which have to travel
with it.

The three scripts in [tools/] of this repository do exactly that for the demo
executable, and are worth reading as a model: [bundle-macos.sh] walks
[otool -L] into a [.app] and ad-hoc signs it, [bundle-linux.sh] walks [ldd] into
a tarball with a launcher that sets [LD_LIBRARY_PATH], and [bundle-windows.sh]
copies the mingw64 DLLs beside the executable.

The one thing to get right is where the art goes, and {!Camlcast.Asset}'s search
order is chosen for exactly those three shapes — see {{!files} Art from files}.

{1 When it goes wrong}

The symptoms, one common cause each, and the step that treats it:

{ul
{li {b The room is black from the inside} — the walls are wound the wrong way
    round; reverse them. The rule is at the top of this page, and
    {!Camlcast.Room.rectangle} and {!Camlcast.Room.regular_polygon} cannot get
    it wrong. (Step 1.)}
{li {b A neighbour arrives mirrored, or inside out} — the doorway's endpoints
    run against the boundary they are cut from; wind them the same way.
    (Step 5.)}
{li {b [World.make] raises [Invalid_argument]} — the level is malformed, and
    the message names the room and the doorway; the list of refusals is in
    step 5.}
{li {b A step catches underfoot at a doorway} — the two floors differ by a few
    thousandths; derive one from the other with {!Camlcast.Plane.through}.
    (Step 6.)}
{li {b The pin fails on macOS} — Homebrew's [sdl2] is [sdl2-compat] and opam
    cannot see it; install the libraries yourself and pass [--no-depexts].
    (Step 0.)}
{li {b A pattern has a hard seam down every wall} — it is built on an
    unwrapped noise; {!Camlcast.Texture.noise} wraps at the texture's size for
    exactly this reason. (Step 2.)}
{li {b A sprite is drawn squashed, or stretched} — a sprite is as wide as its
    {e picture}; give the image the shape you mean the sprite to have.
    (Step 8.)}
{li {b A door you can see through refuses the step} — by design: being able to
    see through a thing is not being able to walk through it. If you meant to
    walk through, the door must stand [Open]. (Step 10.)}
{li {b [of_asset] cannot find a file that exists} — the search is relative to
    the {e executable}, never the source tree; under dune the root is
    [_build/default], and [CAMLCAST_ASSETS] replaces the whole list.
    (Step 11.)}
{li {b Nothing ends the run} — nothing was asked to:
    {!Camlcast.Binding.default} binds no leaving key, and [~leave] is yours to
    fill. (Steps 12 and 13.)}
{li {b Time seems to stop} — the window lost focus; that is the engine's rule,
    not a bug. (Step 12.)}}

{1 Where to read further}

{ul
{li {{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine from scratch} — how the
    picture is actually drawn, derived from first principles.}
{li {!Camlcast.Ray}, {!Camlcast.Plane}, {!Camlcast.Viewport} and
    {!Camlcast.Sky} carry the mathematical derivations in their docstrings.}
{li {!Camlcast.World} carries the portal machinery and the rules a world grows
    by.}
{li The demos: [camlcast-demo] with no argument lists them and
    [camlcast-demo <name>] runs one. Each is a single file with one feature in
    it.}}
